// Lesson 10.1a — 3-Button Jam: Brainstorm & Pitch
// Semester 1 Jam (Jan 4 – Jan 21): 3 buttons only of input across the whole game!
// Expands the Brainstorming & Scope discipline from Unit 8 before writing a single script.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>10.1a — 3-Button Jam: Brainstorm &amp; Pitch</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Welcome back from Winter Break! From now through the end of Semester 1 (January 21), you are
    running your first real game jam. There is only one mechanical constraint: <strong>your entire
    game runs on at most three buttons of input</strong>. Today is all about divergent
    brainstorming, stress-testing ideas against that 3-button rule, and pitching a scope you can
    actually prototype, playtest, and revise in three weeks.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Constraint: Three Buttons Total</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        "Three buttons" means <strong>three discrete inputs total</strong> across the entire active
        game (for example: <code>A</code> + <code>D</code> + <code>Space</code>; <code>Left Arrow</code> +
        <code>Right Arrow</code> + <code>Up Arrow</code>; <code>J</code> + <code>K</code> +
        <code>L</code>; or two keys + left mouse click).
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>No hidden inputs:</strong> WASD is already 4 buttons! Aiming with the mouse cursor while pressing keys counts mouse movement + keys — keep it honestly to three physical presses.</li>
        <li><strong>Why 3 instead of 10?</strong> Fewer buttons force every input to pull double duty (tap vs. hold, press both together, context actions, auto-movement with lane switches or gravity flips). That constraint eliminates clutter and lets testers understand your controls in five seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Menus stay inside the 3 buttons:</strong> Start Game and Retry should use one of your 3 gameplay buttons so a tester never searches the keyboard.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Brainstorm Flood: Five Ideas Before You Pick (~20 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        First ideas are almost always clones of games you already played. Force yourself to sketch
        <strong>five genuinely different 3-button concepts</strong> in 15 minutes before falling in
        love with one:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>#</th><th>Working Idea / Genre</th><th>Button 1</th><th>Button 2</th><th>Button 3</th><th>Why It Could Break</th></tr>
        <tr><td>1</td><td>e.g. Auto-runner + shield + dash</td><td>Jump / Double Jump</td><td>Dash</td><td>Shield / Parry</td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        <strong>Ways to get weird with 3 buttons:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>2-axis spin + fire:</strong> rotate left, rotate right, thrust/shoot forward (classic arcade asteroids).</li>
        <li><strong>3-lane rhythm / catcher:</strong> top lane, middle lane, bottom lane (pressing a button snaps directly to that track!).</li>
        <li><strong>Shared-keyboard 1v1 or co-op?</strong> Two players with only 3 buttons total (1+1+shared switch, or 3 shared actions!).</li>
        <li><strong>Hold vs. Tap / Physics Switch:</strong> button 1 flips gravity, button 2 swaps color/phase, button 3 brakes or fires.</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Filter, Cut Scope &amp; Pitch (~25 min)</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Pick <strong>ONE</strong> winner from your five sketches, run the scope check, and pitch it to
        two classmates before opening Unity:
    </p>

    <ol>
        <li><strong>The Cheat Check:</strong> trace ten seconds of gameplay aloud — if you ever say "and then they press W or aim with the mouse," fix the mechanic or pick another idea from your table.</li>
        <li><strong>One Loop, One Arena:</strong> write down your single win/score condition, single fail condition, and the <em>one</em> thing that escalates difficulty over time (never five separate levels for a two-to-three-week jam).</li>
        <li><strong>Name the Riskiest Part First:</strong> write down which mechanic in your pitch you have NEVER built before — that is the very first thing you greybox next class.</li>
        <li><strong>2-Minute Desk Pitch:</strong> explain your game to two classmates using ONLY: (1) Goal, (2) What Buttons 1, 2, 3 do, and (3) Why it gets tense. Ask them: "Wait — what happens if I press Button 2 twice?" Update your spec on the spot if their question exposes a flaw.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Designing Inside a Constraint</h3>
        <p>What is your committed pitch — Game Title, exact 3 buttons with keys, Win/Score rule, Fail rule — and which of your five brainstormed ideas did you cut and why?</p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Fill out five distinct 3-button concepts without sneaking in a fourth input</li>
        <li>Commit to one scoped core loop with clear win, lose, and escalation rules</li>
        <li>Pitch your 3-button controls cleanly to two classmates and answer their edge-case questions</li>
        <li>Save your pitch notes cleanly in your project repo (and start a fresh Unity scene/repo as instructed)</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-10/index.html">Unit 10: Jam Season</a> — 3-Button Jam schedule &amp; rules</li>
        <li><a href="../projects.html">Projects Overview</a> — how Jam 1 finishes Semester 1</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — input, physics, timers, and prefabs</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="10.1b.html">10.1b — 3-Button Jam: Rapid Prototype</a> — greybox all three buttons fast so you can test the feel today.</p>
</div>`;
---
